#-------------------------------------#
# Fingerprint + Decomposition Testing #
#-------------------------------------#
I am testing a molecular fingerprint + decomposition pipeline with different fingerprints and 2D decomposition algorithms. This will provide a training set whose goal is fit an RNN for predicting the next sequence in a SMILES string such that molecules can be generated and easily characterised by their rarity in relation to the training set. As a result, I want the distribution of molecules in the decomposed space to take on a smooth Guassian character, such that a kernel density estimation will provide a smoothly varying surface that can estimate the rarity of a molecule. I have thought through a testing process for fingerprint + decomposition:
1. Visual distribution
2. Gaussianity tests (skewness, kurtosis, KL divergence)
3. Leave out validation data using both Butina clustered stratified samples and random samples, then see how it is projected in decomposition space
4. Cluster data via kde contours, and check size distribution of clusters
5. Correlation between tanimoto distance in fp space and distance in decomposed space
6. stability under random addition of noise or removal of information from fingerprints, or under removal of density outliers
7. cluster overlap in fingerprint space and decomposition space
8. intrinsic dimensionality prediction

I should also do some common sense checks on how molecules look, chemical intution etc.
Later I could even look at smoothness of physiochemical properties in decomposed space.

Tests 	      	   	   	Markers	 	Description	
Visual distribution		Qualitative	2D scatter, KDE contours
Gaussianity tests		Quantitative	Skew, kurtosis, KL-divergence
Validation projection behavior	Quantitative	Projection drift under Butina/Random split
Cluster analysis		Quantitative	KDE-based cluster size distribution
FP-decomp distance correlation	Quantitative	Spearman/Pearson between Tanimoto and 2D distances
Stability under perturbations	Quantitative	Mean projection shift after noise/dropout
Cluster overlap FP vs decomp	Quantitative	Jaccard index / AMI between cluster labels
Intrinsic dimensionality	Quantitative	PCA / Two-NN / MLE estimates

Try the quantitative assessments for all fingperint-decomposition combos, do visualisations for a few candidates

#-----------------------------------------------------------------#
# RNN training, hyperparameter tuning, random molecule generation #
#-----------------------------------------------------------------#
Then, we use the kde clusters to attempt to even the distribution of randomly generated molecules by the RNN:
- Underepresented class weighting
- SMILES augmentation to balance the training set
- Reinforcment learning (last resort)

RNN hyperparameter tuning (chemts_2_gpu conda env on workstation)
Test the distribution of randomly generated molecules with fingerprint+decomposition embedding

#------#
# MCTS #
#------#
Run MCTS attempting to optimise the rarity of the generated molecule, either based on:
- kernel density estimate of generated molecules on density scale of training set
- maximum distance from maximum density point
- minimum distance to arbitrary points in the decomposed space
- augmentation of training set to bias towards desired molecules
This can then be included as a multiobjective optimisation, multiple MCTS runs to explore the decomposed space, or as post-hoc analysis
